    keyPoints: '- Token bucket: capacity (burst) + refillRate; allow iff at least one token is available after refill\n- Do the whole update — compute elapsed, add tokens, cap at capacity, decrement — inside ONE critical section; that is the thread-safety point\n```kotlin\nclass TokenBucket(private val capacity: Long, private val refillPerSec: Long) {\n    private var tokens = capacity\n    private var lastNs = System.nanoTime()\n    @Synchronized fun tryAcquire(): Boolean {\n        val now = System.nanoTime()\n        tokens = minOf(capacity, tokens + (now - lastNs) * refillPerSec / 1_000_000_000L)\n        lastNs = now\n        if (tokens < 1) return false\n        tokens--\n        return true\n    }\n}\n```\n- Lock-free variant: pack (tokens, timestamp) in one long and CAS-loop, or use a Semaphore whose permits refill on a scheduler\n- Compare algorithms: fixed window (cheap, burst at boundary), sliding log (exact, heavy), sliding counter (approx), leaky bucket (smooths output) — token bucket allows controlled bursts\n- Mention multi-instance reality: local bucket is per-process; global needs Redis or a sidecar',
