    related: [ 'ds-5', 'ds-6', 'ds-7' ],
    source: 'interview',
    complexity: '- Time: O(n·d) for the walk-to-tail version, O(n) for the stack/recursion version (n = total nodes, d = nesting depth)\n- Space: O(1) walk-to-tail; O(d) for the explicit stack or recursion frames',
    redFlags: '- Overwrites `cur.next` before saving it, losing the rest of the current level\n- Links the child list forward only, so `prev` pointers are wrong in a doubly linked list\n- Recursive version returns nothing, so the caller cannot re-attach the sibling it saved\n- Leaves `child` set on flattened nodes',
    keyPoints: '- Node carries prev, next AND child; flattening = depth-first splice: the whole child list goes between `cur` and the saved `cur.next`\n- Save `nxt = cur.next` BEFORE touching any pointer, then re-attach it at the child list\'s tail\n```kotlin\nfun flatten(head: Node?): Node? {\n    var cur = head\n    while (cur != null) {\n        if (cur.child != null) {\n            val nxt = cur.next\n            cur.next = cur.child; cur.child!!.prev = cur; cur.child = null\n            var tail = cur\n            while (tail.next != null) tail = tail.next!!   // walk to child-list tail\n            tail.next = nxt; nxt?.prev = tail\n        }\n        cur = cur.next\n    }\n    return head\n}\n```\n- Ordering rule: a node\'s own `next` comes AFTER its whole child subtree — the preorder of a tree where "left" = child and "right" = next\n- Stack form of the same DFS: when a child exists, push `nxt` then link the child in; pop when a list runs out — every push is a level you must return to, so the order is exactly preorder\n- The walk-to-tail version is O(1) space but O(n·d) time; deep single-node children make it quadratic. The stack version is linear with O(d) space — say both, then pick\n- Recursive contract that actually works: `flatten(child)` RETURNS the child list\'s tail so the caller can attach `nxt`; `child = null` on the way out',
    answer: '- For each node with a child: save `next`, splice the child list in with both `next` and `prev` wired, clear `child`, then attach the saved node at the child list\'s tail.\n- Preorder is the rule — child subtree first, then the sibling — and it is DFS on a tree whose left is child and right is next.\n- Walk-to-tail is `O(1)` space but `O(n·d)` time; an explicit stack or a recursion that returns the tail gives `O(n)` with `O(d)` space.'
