    title: 'Design an LFU cache — least-frequently-used eviction with O(1) get and put (LC 460).',
    tags: [ 'hash-map', 'design', 'doubly-linked-list', 'cache-eviction' ],
    related: [ 'ds-23', 'ds-31', 'ds-10' ],
    source: 'interview',
    complexity: '- Time: O(1) get and put — hash lookups plus constant-time list remove/addFirst\n- Space: O(capacity) nodes + O(distinct frequencies) buckets, bounded by O(capacity)',
    followUp: '**Follow-up:** When does LFU beat LRU, and what extra policy does it need to stay usable?\n> LFU keeps entries that are hot but idle and drops cold-but-recent ones — good for stable "hit-item" workloads (recommendation lists, config), bad for load spikes. Real caches add AGING: decay counts periodically, or use a window (W-TinyLFU / LIRS) so frequency is measured over a recent interval. Also state the tiebreak: LFU without recency-as-tiebreak evicts arbitrarily among equals.',
    redFlags: '- Searches all entries for the minimum frequency, making eviction O(n)\n- Sorts entries by count on each access instead of moving a node between buckets\n- Forgets the recency tiebreak inside a frequency bucket\n- Does not reset minFreq when the min bucket empties, or on insert, and then evicts from the wrong list',
    keyPoints: '- LRU (`ds-23`) orders by recency; LFU orders by access COUNT with least-recent-used as tiebreak. Keeping both O(1) is the whole problem\n- Layout: `map: key → Node(value, freq)`, plus one bucket per frequency — a doubly linked list in recency order, newest at the front — plus a `minFreq` field\n- A touch removes the node from bucket f and adds it at the front of bucket f+1; if bucket f is now empty and f == minFreq, minFreq becomes f+1\n- Insert of a NEW key always starts at freq 1 and resets minFreq = 1; eviction is then the TAIL of bucket minFreq, which is why no search is needed\n```kotlin\nclass LFUCache(val capacity: Int) {\n    class Node(val key: Int, var value: Int, var freq: Int = 1)\n    private val map = HashMap<Int, Node>()\n    private val buckets = HashMap<Int, ArrayDeque<Node>>() // front = most recent\n    private var minFreq = 0\n    fun get(key: Int): Int { val n = map[key] ?: return -1; return touch(n).value }\n    fun put(key: Int, value: Int) {\n        if (capacity == 0) return\n        map[key]?.let { it.value = value; touch(it); return }\n        if (map.size == capacity) map.remove(buckets.getValue(minFreq).removeLast().key)\n        val n = Node(key, value)\n        map[key] = n; buckets.getOrPut(1) { ArrayDeque() }.addFirst(n); minFreq = 1\n    }\n    private fun touch(n: Node): Node {\n        val dq = buckets.getValue(n.freq); dq.remove(n)\n        if (dq.isEmpty()) { buckets.remove(n.freq); if (minFreq == n.freq) minFreq = n.freq + 1 }\n        n.freq++\n        buckets.getOrPut(n.freq) { ArrayDeque() }.addFirst(n)\n        return n\n    }\n}\n```\n- Why the node lives in ONE bucket at a time and is removed by reference: that is what makes the bucket move O(1) (Java `LinkedHashMap` or a `list::splice` in C++ give the same guarantee)\n- Contrast the cousin designs: TTL + LRU eviction reuses this exact shape but orders by expiry time (a time-ordered queue, or lazy validation on read), not by count\n- Interviewers accept "frequency with aging" as the real-world answer; they reject the version that never decays counts and therefore never evicts an old hit item',
    answer: '- `key → Node(value, freq)` plus one recency-ordered bucket list per frequency, and a `minFreq` pointer; a touch moves the node from bucket f to the front of bucket f+1.\n- Insert sets `minFreq = 1`; an emptied min bucket bumps `minFreq`. Evicting is the tail of `minFreq` — constant time because it is tracked, never searched.\n- `O(1)` get/put with `O(capacity)` space; LFU needs one more index than LRU and an aging policy to stay useful.'
