    redFlags: '- Divides the total product by a[i] and cannot handle one zero, let alone two\n- Allocates prefix AND suffix arrays when the output can hold one side\n- Multiplies a[i] into its own answer (off-by-one on the running product)\n- Never states what changes under overflow or modular arithmetic',
    keyPoints: '- The answer is `out[i] = (product of everything strictly left of i) × (product of everything strictly right of i)` — prefix and suffix products of the same array\n- Pass 1 writes the running left product into out[i] BEFORE consuming nums[i]; pass 2 walks back multiplying in the running right product, so no extra array is needed\n```kotlin\nfun productExceptSelf(nums: IntArray): IntArray {\n    val out = IntArray(nums.size) { 1 }\n    var left = 1\n    for (i in nums.indices) { out[i] = left; left *= nums[i] }\n    var right = 1\n    for (i in nums.indices.reversed()) { out[i] *= right; right *= nums[i] }\n    return out\n}\n```\n- Division (`total / nums[i]`) is the tempting wrong answer: it dies on a zero, and patching it needs a zero COUNT — one zero makes exactly one position non-zero, two or more make all zero\n- LC 238 excludes the output array from the space bound, which is why the second pass multiplies INTO out instead of using another buffer; say that explicitly, it is the point of the question\n- Same prefix/suffix family: trapping rain water (`ds-29`) is min(leftMax)/rightMax, product of the last k numbers, range-sum/range-product queries after point updates (prefix arrays, then a Fenwick tree)\n- Overflow: 10^5 values near 10^9 blow past Long — ask for the modulus (then no division without a modular inverse, and zeros break that too) or BigInteger\n- Order of the two passes is interchangeable; running both in a single loop with two accumulators is the O(n)/O(1) flourish some interviewers ask for',
    answer: '- Two sweeps: left-to-right writes the running product of everything before i; right-to-left multiplies in the running product of everything after i.\n- `O(n)` time, `O(1)` extra space because the output array carries the prefix side — that exclusion is the trick the question is testing.\n- Total÷a[i] fails on zeros and needs a zero-count special case; prefix/suffix never divides and generalizes to `ds-29`-style left/right sweeps.'
